import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'..')
from con_helpers import *
save(2,2,"The Inner Instrument: Four Workers in One Mind","When something inside gets difficult this week, do not say my mind is a mess. Ask: which worker is this, manas, buddhi, ahaṁkāra or chitta? Naming the worker is most of the work.",[
mc("U","Kai completes “My mind is...” with racing, blank, and “can’t make up.” What does Swamiji draw from the three?",
 ["They are three different operations behind one word: too much movement, none, and trouble deciding.","They are three words for one operation, so the mind is a single thing that simply has different moods.","They are three symptoms of one illness, so the mind needs one remedy that deals with all of them at once.","They are three faults of language only, so the mind itself has no parts and nothing that can pull apart."],0,
 "We use one word for a crowd of different operations, and they do not always agree. James decides how to handle a student and a second later a voice says it is the wrong call: one part decided and another doubted, so it is a system with parts that can pull against each other.",
 "One word, many operations","Part 1"),
mc("U","What does antaḥkaraṇa mean, and how many working parts does the tradition map in it?",
 ["The inner instrument, mapped into four working parts.","The inner witness, mapped into three working parts.","The outer world, mapped into five working parts.","The body’s organs, mapped into six working parts."],0,
 "Antaḥ means inner and karaṇa means instrument or tool. The four are not four organs in different parts of the brain but four jobs in one stream, like a river carrying fast surface currents and slow deep ones.",
 "Antaḥkaraṇa: four jobs in one stream","Part 1"),
mc("A","A man says “I cannot switch off my thoughts,” and another says “this is happening to me.” Which workers are they describing?",
 ["Manas, the restless processing radar, and ahaṁkāra, the I-maker that stamps it mine.","Buddhi, the decider that resolves the meaning, and chitta, the memory-field that stores it.","Chitta, the memory-field that stores it, and manas, the restless processing radar of the mind.","Ahaṁkāra, the I-maker that stamps it mine, and buddhi, the decider that resolves the meaning."],0,
 "Manas takes in what arrives and asks whether it is this or that, left or right, safe or dangerous. Buddhi resolves: this is what it is, this is what to do. Ahaṁkāra makes it personal: the difference between it is raining and my picnic is ruined. Chitta is the ground where every experience leaves a trace.",
 "Manas processes, ahaṁkāra owns","Part 1"),
mc("A","James’s phone buzzes with “Can we have a word tomorrow?” and he feels instant dread. Which worker turns six neutral words into a threat?",
 ["Buddhi, the decider, which assigns the meaning “this is a threat.”","Manas, the radar, which detects the signal and pulls his attention over to the phone.","Ahaṁkāra, the I-maker, which adds the self-tagging that makes it about his own job.","Chitta, the memory-field, which stores the unease that colours the whole evening for him."],0,
 "Swamiji says this is the hinge: if buddhi had resolved it as she probably wants to praise that project, the evening would go a different way, though the six words are the same. Sarah sees that much of her worry is her buddhi picking the dark reading.",
 "Buddhi assigns the dark meaning","Part 2"),
judge("“The inner instrument only processes the present moment, so a worrying moment has no effect on the next one.”",F,
 "Swamiji says the instrument is also conditioning the future. The impression is stored by chitta and drops into an existing groove, so next time manas is jumpier, buddhi reaches for the dark reading faster and ahaṁkāra stings harder. The groove is laid by repetition.",
 "The instrument conditions the future","Part 2"),
mc("A","Sarah says a worry habit feels as if the track is already laid. What does Swamiji say about this?",
 ["It is laid by repetition, and the same loop that builds the groove can be watched, so the loop loosens.","It is laid by birth, and so nothing a person does can ever loosen the groove or change the track.","It is laid by other people, so only a change in the people around her can loosen the groove.","It is laid by chance, so the groove will loosen by itself if she waits long enough for it."],0,
 "The moment you can watch manas jump, buddhi reach for threat and ahaṁkāra make it about you, and realise you are the one watching, the loop loosens, not instantly but it loosens. The mechanism that builds the cage is the same one that opens it.",
 "The loop that builds the groove can be seen","Part 2"),
mc("A","Kai asks which of the four workers is “me.” What does Swamiji answer?",
 ["None of them: all four are weather, and you are the awareness they are happening in.","Buddhi, since the decider is the part of the mind that is closest to the true Self in a person.","Ahaṁkāra, since the I-maker is the part of the mind that really says who a person is.","All four together, since a committee of workers is just what a person is, and nothing more."],0,
 "The inner instrument is closer to you than your own breath but is not you in the deepest sense. A telescope reveals stars but is not the eye; a violin makes music but is not the musician. Moving from I am anxious to anxiety is here, in the instrument, and I notice it opens a space.",
 "The instrument is not you","Part 3"),
judge("“Swamiji teaches that the mind is an enemy to be hated and escaped, with a pure Self above and a nasty mind below.”",F,
 "Sarah’s worry about coldness is answered with Vivekananda: body, mind and Self are not three things stacked up but one reality seen at different levels of clarity. The figure in the mist that seemed a stranger is your friend dimly seen. The mind is the Self seen foggily; the waves are the ocean moving.",
 "The mind is the Self seen foggily","Part 3"),
mc("A","In the Kaṭha Upaniṣad chariot, the charioteer is clear but the reins are slack. What happens, and what does this show?",
 ["The horses bolt: even a clear buddhi cannot steer if manas is not held, so decision needs attention to reach the body.","The chariot stops: a clear buddhi needs no reins, since the Self in the seat can steer by thought alone.","The horses obey: a clear buddhi is enough to rule the senses, whatever the state of the reins at the time.","The rider takes over: the Self drives the horses directly, since the charioteer is of no use at all."],0,
 "The chariot is the body, the horses the senses, the reins manas, the charioteer buddhi and the rider the Self. Kai’s image is a charioteer shouting at horses with the reins dropped. This is why just decide to be different so often fails.",
 "Decision needs attention to reach the body","Part 3"),
mc("A","Kai’s attention is shot and he cannot focus. What does the practical experiment suggest?",
 ["Diagnose, do not moralise: this is manas, untrained, off the leash; then pick up the reins with one breath, one object.","Moralise, so as to shame the mind into focus: a firm word with himself should bring the attention back to the task.","Wait, since attention returns of its own accord, and any effort to steady it only makes the scattering worse.","Give up the task, since a scattered mind cannot be steadied and it is better not to try until another day."],0,
 "He is not fixing his whole self, just steadying one worker. Sarah is to ask whether her manas is spinning, her buddhi has decided something dark, or her ahaṁkāra is making it about her adequacy as a mother; James to move from the stung I back to buddhi: what does this group actually need?",
 "Name the worker and steady it","Practical situation")])
